INT. ROLLER RINK, AFTER CLOSING - NIGHT

Mirror ball stopped. House lights half up. Rental skates in cubbies. A snack bar with its grill off.

THEODORA "TEDDY" MWANGI (11) sits on the rail, one skate on, one off. Rink floor gleams.

Her father, AUGUSTIN MWANGI (40s, rink manager, keys on a belt loop), mops the far lane.

Her aunt FAYE SOLOMON-DRAKE (30s, coat still on, car keys in fist) walks in from the lobby.

FAYE
Door was open.

AUGUSTIN
I'm still here.

He keeps mopping. Faye watches the bucket.

FAYE
Teddy. Shoes.

Teddy doesn't move.

FAYE (CONT'D)
Your dad and I need a minute.

TEDDY
You can say it in front of me.

AUGUSTIN
Teddy.

TEDDY
I'm eleven, not stupid.

Faye crosses to the rail. Sits one spot down. Close, not close enough.

FAYE
The offer's still open. The spare room. The school's good.

AUGUSTIN
Her school's good here.

FAYE
It's two hours away.

AUGUSTIN
It's my kid.

He stops mopping. Leans the handle on the wall.

Teddy pulls her second skate off. Sets it beside the first. Lines them up, toes touching.

TEDDY
Do I get a vote?

FAYE
Of course you do.

AUGUSTIN
Of course.

Neither of them looks at her.

TEDDY
Mom's skates are still in the office.

Augustin and Faye both freeze. Faye stares at the floor. Augustin studies the ceiling.

AUGUSTIN
They don't fit anyone.

TEDDY
They fit me.

Faye lets out a breath. Almost a laugh.

FAYE
Size five. She had tiny feet.

Augustin walks to the snack bar. Pours three cups of water from the tap. Brings them over. Hands one to each.

They drink. The ice machine clunks.

TEDDY
Can I skate once? Alone? Then we decide.

Augustin looks at Faye. Faye looks at Augustin. Slowly, both nod.

Teddy slides off the rail in her socks. Walks to the center of the empty rink.

She doesn't skate. She just stands there. Arms out. Eyes closed.

Faye puts her cup down. Augustin sits.

They wait.
